----------------------------------------------------------\nCalcium - Chandra (https://www.imsc.res.in/cgi-bin/CalciumShyam/Calcium40.pl?Amount=Year&CalendarName=MathematicsEvents&Date=2019%2F10%2F25&DoneURL=Calcium40.pl%3FCalendarName%3DMathematicsEvents%26Op%3DShowIt%26Amount%3DYear%26NavType%3DNeither%26Type%3DList%26Date%3D2019%252F1%252F1&ID=712&NavType=Neither&Op=PopupWindow&Source=Chandra&Type=List)\nciteturn2search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.0 years ago; Crawled: last month; #### Jayanthan A V ... Raghavan generalized the concept of d-sequences to quadratic sequences.\nFriday, October 25 2019\n10:00 - 11:00\n\n#### Chandrasekhar Hall\n\n#### d-sequences, quadratic sequences, their Rees algebras and Castelnuovo-Mumford regularities.\n\n#### Jayanthan A V\n\n##### IIT Madras\n\n#### d-sequences were introduced by Huneke and he used the theory of d-sequences to study depth(R/I^n). Raghavan generalized the concept of d-sequences to quadratic sequences. We study certain binomial ideals, generated by a subset of 2-minors of a 2xn matrix of variables, their Rees algebras, certain first Betti numbers and first syzygies. We also study of the Castelnuovo-Mumford regularity of powers of homogeneous quadratic sequences in polynomial rings and use them to obtain the regularity of powers of some of the d-sequence binomial ideals.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA simple proof that ideals generated by d-sequences are of linear type (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/233193919_A_simple_proof_that_ideals_generated_by_d-sequences_are_of_linear_type)\nciteturn2search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 19.4 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Raghavan ... In this paper, we investigate the conjecture put forth by Jayanthan, Kumar, and Sarkar (2021) that if G is a tree or a unicyclic graph, then the binomial edge ideal of G is of linear type.\n  * Neeraj Kumar\n\nAn ideal I of a commutative ring R is said to be of {\\emph{linear type}} when its Rees algebra and symmetric algebra exhibit isomorphism. In this paper, we investigate the conjecture put forth by Jayanthan, Kumar, and Sarkar (2021) that if G is a tree or a unicyclic graph, then the binomial edge ideal of G is of linear type. Our investigation validates this conjecture for trees. However, our study reveals that not all unicyclic graphs adhere to this conjecture. Furthermore, we provide an explicit description of the defining ideal of the Rees algebra of binomial edge ideals of trees and demonstrate that these algebras are Cohen-Macaulay. Finally, we conclude the equality between symbolic powers and ordinary powers of binomial edge ideals of trees.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\n... In general, it is quite a hard task to describe the defining ideals of Rees algebras. Huneke proved that the defining ideal of the Rees algebra of an ideal generated by a d-sequence has a linear generating set, [11] (see [21] for a simple proof). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nresearchseminars.org - View series (https://researchseminars.org/seminar/VCAS)\nciteturn2search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Raghavan  | Multiplicities of points on Schubert varieties in the Grassmannian-II ... Fri  | Sep 18  | 13:30  | Ben Briggs  | On a conjecture of Vasconcelos - Part 2\nFri  | Oct 09  | 12:00  | Sarang Sane  | $K_0$ and ideals\nTue  | Oct 06  | 12:00  | Mrinal Das  | Some open problems in projective modules and complete intersections\nFri  | Oct 02  | 12:00  | K. N. Raghavan  | Multiplicities of points on Schubert varieties in the Grassmannian-II\nTue  | Sep 29  | 12:00  | K. N. Raghavan  | Multiplicities of points on Schubert varieties in the Grassmannian-I\nFri  | Sep 25  | 12:00  | Shunsuke Takagi  | TBA\nTue  | Sep 22  | 12:00  | Shunsuke Takagi  | $F$-singularities and singularities in birational geometry - Part 1\nFri  | Sep 18  | 13:30  | Ben Briggs  | On a conjecture of Vasconcelos - Part 2\